WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.760
Thanks to Brilliant for sponsoring this episode.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.040
Hey Crazies.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.580
Mirrors are useful tools, especially in the bathroom.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.220
They seem like they’re pretty easy to understand.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.520
Or are they?!

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.760
Rule number 8 here in the asylum is question everything.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.880
You think you understand mirrors,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.200
but all it takes it takes is just the right question to destroy everything you think you know.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.900
So let’s start with the stuff everyone knows.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.820
Mirrors flip things left-to-right, which makes those things look backward.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.700
The you that you see in the mirror is not the you that other people see when they look at you.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.380
Here’s what Albert Einstein looked like.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.080
This is how he looked to himself in the mirror.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.420
They’re backwards from each other.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.520
It’s at least part of the reason that many people don’t like pictures or video of themselves.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.660
That’s just not how we’re used to seeing our own faces.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.700
You’re used to seeing your own face the other way around

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.100
and, since no one is perfectly symmetric, our subconscious notices the difference,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.620
even if our conscious mind can’t quite tell what’s wrong.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.780
You can get used to it though.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.480
After years of staring at myself like this, I’ve gotten comfortable with both directions.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.480
What’s so crazy about all that though?

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.100
Well, I lied a little bit.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.320
Mirrors don’t actually flip anything.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.640
You heard me right!

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.260
Mirrors don’t flip. The reflect.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.600
Your brain just thinks it’s a flip.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.900
Back at the mirror, my clone was holding a piece of paper with the word “things” on it.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.420
It looks backward from how you would normally read the word.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.920
But the word already isn’t facing you.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.620
It’s facing the mirror.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.760
If you could see the word through the paper,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.720
you’d see the reflection is actually the same direction as the word.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.920
If you flip the paper around so the word is facing you,

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.660
the word in the reflection is also the right way.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:51.940
You can read it.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.220
The light just goes to the mirror and comes back.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.640
There is no flip.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.720
The real problem here is your expectations.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.180
And those aren’t going to serve you very well over the next few minutes.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.060
Speaking of expectations,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.820
in this painting, a little girl is looking at herself in a mirror.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:09.760
Right?

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.860
Wrong!

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.440
She’s looking at you.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.900
Mirrors let us see ourselves, but only if we’re looking directly at them.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.240
The direction a mirror faces is given by an arrow or line called the normal,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.420
which just means that it makes a 90 degree angle with the surface of the mirror.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.820
It’s from the old Latin meaning of the word.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.780
We measure all angles from that normal line.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.660
See, if light shines on a mirror, it reflects off at very specific angle:

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.280
The angle it came in at, but on the opposite side of that normal line.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.999
These two angles must be the same.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.800
It’s called the law of reflection and it’s true for any incoming angle.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.440
Even if we curve the mirror, the law of reflection must be obeyed.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.840
So let’s take another look at that painting.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.140
This little girl is not directly facing her mirror.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.220
The mirror is at an angle.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.620
If she’s seeing light coming from the mirror,

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.660
according to the law of reflection, that light has to come from a different place.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.480
It came from the front of the painting where we happen to be standing.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.960
We can see her in the mirror, but she sees us.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.220
I can easily demonstrate this with my own mirror.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.380
But why though? Why does that law look that way?

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.900
Why is the reflected angle always the same as the incoming angle?

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.020
Um, it just is?

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.180
OK OK, I’ll give this a shot.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.980
We need to go deep for this, so don’t say I didn’t warn you.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:37.240
To even begin to answer this, we need more than just light rays.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.820
Light is disturbance in the electromagnetic or EM field.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.360
It’s a wave.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.000
These disturbances move outward from whatever source is causing them

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.120
and they move at the speed of light.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.440
They are light.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.480
But having all those arrows, exes, and dots all over the place is going to get confusing.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.940
Instead, let’s abstract all that away and just keep track of the wave peaks.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.760
Think of these like the water waves you’d see approaching a shore line.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.620
They’re called wave fronts and they’re going to help us keep things simple.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.280
Now let’s look at what happens when one these wave fronts hits a mirror.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.380
If the wave front comes in at an angle, one end of it will hit the mirror first.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.740
That part will reflect before the rest of the wave front.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.200
To understand why that reflection even happens, we need to look even closer.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.620
Super zoom!

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.060
The mirror’s surface is covered in silver atoms.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.600
I know it’s not always silver. Just go with it.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.820
Each of those silver atoms is made of a positively-charged nucleus

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:41.140
surrounded by a cloud of negatively-charged electrons.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.820
And, in case you didn’t know, charges are affected by the EM field.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.380
When the wave front hits an atom, the charges inside that atom begin to vibrate.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:54.600
That vibrating charge makes its own light that goes out in all directions.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.940
Here’s the thing though:

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:02.420
While each atom creates it’s own disturbances in the EM field, there is only one EM field,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.060
so it can only respond to the total of all the disturbances together.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.340
Many of those disturbances are going to be out of sync.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.140
They’ll cancel each other out.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:17.440
It just so happens those disturbances cancel everywhere except one specific angle:

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.140
The angle predicted by the law of reflection.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.820
How cool is that!?!

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.960
But if we’re looking at atoms, shouldn’t we be using quantum mechanics?

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.440
I can’t get away with anything around here, can I?

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.680
Alight, so I cheated a little.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:35.660
This method is called Huygens principle and was developed all the way back in 1678.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.880
It was updated in 1818, but, needless to say, it’s a bit old.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.980
If we’re going to explain the law of reflection with quantum mechanics,

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.860
we need to reframe the question a little.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.420
Say we’ve got a laser that can point in any direction we’d like,

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.100
but we need it to get to some detector.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.420
Just for fun, we’ll make the detector a micro black hole.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.900
If light goes in, the black hole grows and we count that as a detection.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.620
It’s a boring example, OK? I’ve got to spice it up somehow.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.600
Now let’s put a wall in the way, so the laser can’t go directly to the black hole.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.860
We need the light to go around the wall.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.980
That’s where our mirror comes in.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.960
We can reflect the light off of the mirror and get it to our black hole.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.740
If you’ve been paying attention, you know the angles must be equal,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.760
so the light has to hit the mirror at a very specific place to reach the black hole.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.300
Or does it?!

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.260
Instead of imagining this laser beam as a ray or a wave,

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.180
we’re going to imagine it as a stream of photons:

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.400
little packets of probability.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:40.400
If one of those photons enters our black hole, it’ll grow.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.620
To keep things simple, we’re only going to consider one photon at a time.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:48.440
This photon exits the laser and hits the mirror.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.660
Our expectation is that the photon should obey the law of reflection,

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.320
but that’s not necessarily what happens.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.800
What did I tell you earlier about your expectations?

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.080
Let them go!

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:04.140
Our photon knows nothing about the law of reflection and neither does the atom it hits.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:08.360
If a photon exits a laser and enters our micro black hole,

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.520
there’s a chance it could have taken any path between them,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.020
even if those paths don’t obey the law of reflection.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:18.160
Does it take all the available paths simultaneously?

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.260
Yes.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.480
Kind of.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.040
Well, not really.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.180
Eh, maybe.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:24.740
I hate quantum mechanics.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.500
What’s physically happening depends on your favorite interpretation.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:33.720
All we know for sure, is that we must consider all the paths in our calculation.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.600
“Shut up and calculate” is a famous motto in quantum mechanics.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.640
If we want to know the probability that a photon will reach the black hole,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.680
then all these paths contribute something to that probability,

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.940
even if those paths don’t obey the law of reflection.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:52.760
We just need to figure out how much each of those paths contribute.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.080
We could do the math outright, adding up exponentials like this,

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.700
but we’re not going to.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.920
I mean, why do math with symbols when you can do it visually instead?

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:06.860
Let’s imagine this thing is just an arrow with a particular length,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:11.040
something we’d call a phase vector or phasor for short.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.860
Over time, the arrow spins around at a pace given by the frequency of the photon.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:22.120
Visible light frequencies are pretty high though, so this arrow is zipping around like crazy.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.100
Zoom zoom zoom zoom zoom zoom zoom.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:29.040
But, ultimately, each available path for the photon takes a different amount of time,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.140
so those spinning arrows end up in different orientations when they’re done.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:38.440
Remember though, these arrows are a visual way of representing the photon’s wave.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.300
A wave of probability.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:44.300
To find the total probability the photon will arrive at the black hole,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.700
we need to add all these arrows together.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.420
Visually, we just put all the arrows in order, tail to head,

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.820
because that’s how you add vectors together.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.440
The arrows caught up in the spirals tend to cancel themselves out,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.260
so only the arrows near the center contribute to the probability.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.520
That’s exactly the location we’d expect given the law of reflection.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.440
The law of reflection just comes from the probabilities of photons.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.520
It’s just probabilities.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.220
But that’s just a math trick, right?

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:15.180
The photon is really only taking the middle path, right?!

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.480
Nope! The photon interacts with the entire mirror and I can prove it.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:23.140
If we exclude the middle path by removing some mirror,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.660
the law of reflection tells us that no photons should reach the black hole,

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.300
but some of them do.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:34.180
Some of the remaining paths still create a non-zero probability.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.920
That single photon still interacts with the other parts of the mirror, like, for real,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.660
even on the edges!

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.680
Let’s say we only have a mirrored surface on the far left edge of the screen.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.500
That way we’re only including a spiral of cancelling arrows.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.960
In that case, as you’d expect, we do not receive a photon at the black hole,

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.180
unless we tweak the mirror.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.170
Let’s say we know the locations where the quantum arrows point left

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:02.300
and we remove those sections of the mirror as well.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.940
Now the arrows don’t cancel in a spiral.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:09.260
Even though none of the paths are even remotely obeying the law of reflection,

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:12.600
they add up to a non-zero probability anyway.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.900
The photon can still arrive at the black hole.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:19.700
Classically, these situations would be called diffraction,

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.960
but, in quantum mechanics, it’s just probabilities.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:26.800
Mirrors violate our expectations at every turn.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.160
Sometimes, we can figure it out if we draw a ray diagram.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:33.400
Other times, we need to consider the wave nature of light.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.740
But, in some circumstances, not even that will work.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.320
That’s when we resort to quantum mechanics

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.820
because reflection is ultimately probabilistic.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:47.480
The point is, we didn’t really understand mirrors until the last century.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.040
Don’t feel too bad if your instincts failed you today.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:53.160
So, do you feel like you understand mirrors a little better?

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:54.780
Let us know in the comments.

